        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The samples requested from STS-135 will be used to examine further changes in thymus and immune cells of flown mice. Excellent results have resulted from our initial experiment on STS-131. However, more sample is needed for follow-up to fully complete the study. First, we need to have more data on the thymus, e.g. protein composition of the thymus may be altered in spaceflight since a difference in texture of the thymus was noted, but we do not know the cause. We would send the thymus out for fat analysis, and in house do key protein content of the organ as well as verify the changes in RNA induction. Secondly, we request spleen samples to verify RNA T-cell induction data collected on STS-131 as well as collection of protein from the spleens to investigate target proteins suggested from the STS-131 astro mice.<P>

APPROACH:<br>
The objective of this study was to evaluate the effect of spaceflight on early T cell activation.  C57BL/6J female mice were exposed to microgravity for 15 days on STS-131. Splenocytes were harvested within 2-3 hrs post-flight, cells isolated and activated (~9 hrs after landing) with Concanavalin A and antiCD28 for 2.5 hrs. Cells were harvested (11.5 hrs post landing) and stored in RNAprotect (Qiagen) at -80°C. Total RNA was isolated from the activated samples with RNeasy (Qiagen).<P>

RESULTS:<br>
Due to limited initial sample size our results were not statistically complete. However flight results on STS-131 were very successful and the results are being prepared for publication.<P>
        </div>

